mimalloc as libroot's allocator (experimental, off by default)
==============================================================

This directory holds mimalloc 3.2.8 (the copy WebKit bundles in
Source/bmalloc/mimalloc, MIT license, see mimalloc/LICENSE) and the glue that
makes it the malloc() of libroot.so. The default allocator stays the OpenBSD
one in ../openbsd. Background and measurements: airos-works/haiku#75.


Building
--------

Set, for example in the build directory's UserBuildConfig:

	HAIKU_LIBROOT_MALLOC = mimalloc ;

jam does not notice that the switch changed. After changing it, remove
libroot.so from the build directory
(objects/haiku/<arch>/release/system/libroot/libroot.so) and build again.
libroot_debug.so is not affected: it keeps its debug heaps.

Only 64 bit targets (x86_64, arm64): the glue reads the thread pointer with
__builtin_thread_pointer(), and mimalloc needs C11 atomics, which rules out
the gcc2 half of an x86_gcc2 hybrid.


What is where
-------------

libroot_malloc.c
	The malloc() family (malloc, calloc, realloc, free, posix_memalign,
	aligned_alloc, memalign, valloc, malloc_usable_size) and the hooks
	libroot calls: __init_heap, __heap_thread_init, __heap_thread_exit,
	__heap_before_fork, __heap_after_fork_parent, __heap_after_fork_child,
	__heap_terminate_after. It includes all of mimalloc (mimalloc/src/static.c)
	and is compiled with hidden visibility: libroot exports exactly the
	symbols it exported before, minus the OpenBSD allocator's private page
	allocator (__allocate_pages and friends). No mi_*() function is exported,
	so an application that brings its own mimalloc keeps using its own.

mimalloc/src/prim/haiku/prim.c
	The operating system primitives. (mimalloc's own unix/prim.c is what an
	application's copy of mimalloc uses on Haiku.)

mimalloc/
	The imported sources. Changes against the import are all marked with
	MI_HAIKU_LIBROOT or a "haiku:" comment:
	- include/mimalloc/prim.h, src/init.c, src/threadlocal.c, src/options.c:
	  no ELF thread local storage; the per thread pointers live in four
	  fixed TLS slots (headers/private/system/tls.h).
	- src/prim/prim.c: selects haiku/prim.c.
	- src/alloc.c: no weak fallback for std::get_new_handler(). As a hidden
	  symbol it took libroot's real one out of the exports.
	- src/page.c: a purge that is due runs with the next allocation that
	  leaves the fast path.
	- src/arena.c: a visit of all arenas no longer forgets purges that are
	  not due yet (upstream bug: after that only a forced collection returned
	  the memory).


Design notes
------------

Thread local storage. ELF TLS in libroot goes through the runtime loader,
which allocates the blocks on first use and frees them at thread exit before
libroot's heap hook runs. The allocator uses kernel TLS slots instead: one
load from the thread pointer, no initialisation, valid for the whole life of
the thread and inherited by fork(). __heap_thread_init() stores mimalloc's
empty heap in the slots: mimalloc's "slot starts as NULL" model crashed in
realloc() when that was the first call of a thread.

Memory accounting. The kernel does not overcommit ordinary areas. Two modes
(see prim.c):
- overcommit (default): arenas are mapped read/write with MAP_NORESERVE. The
  kernel commits each page when it is first touched and releases it on
  MADV_FREE. No system call to commit, and fork() is as fast as before. When
  memory runs out the touch fails (the program crashes) where malloc() would
  have returned NULL.
- strict (MIMALLOC_HAIKU_OVERCOMMIT=0 in the environment, or build with
  -DMI_HAIKU_OVERCOMMIT=0): arenas are reserved PROT_NONE and committed with
  mprotect(). malloc() returns NULL when memory runs out, but the areas get
  a protection per page: fork() of a 256 MiB heap took 20 ms instead of 2 ms
  (5.5 ms with the vm_copy_on_write_area() change that came with this).

Arena size. Address space is reserved 64 MiB at a time, not 1 GiB: in strict
mode the kernel allocates 4 bits per page of an area with mixed protections
(128 KiB per GiB, in every team).

Memory return. Freed pages are given back with MADV_FREE one second after
they became free (mimalloc's purge delay), when the next allocation leaves
the fast path or the next page becomes free. A program that frees a lot and
then does not allocate at all keeps the memory until it allocates again:
there is no timer. MIMALLOC_PURGE_DELAY=0 returns memory at once, at a cost
(see the measurements).

fork(). The hooks take mimalloc's eight locks before the fork and release
them in the parent; the child sets them up again. The heaps of the threads
that do not exist in the child stay as they were: their blocks are valid and
can be freed, but that memory is not reused in the child.

Environment. mimalloc's MIMALLOC_* variables work (MIMALLOC_VERBOSE=1,
MIMALLOC_SHOW_STATS=1, MIMALLOC_PURGE_DELAY=<ms>, ...).


Known differences and open points
---------------------------------

- malloc(n) with n <= 8 returns memory aligned to 8 bytes (16 for everything
  larger). The OpenBSD allocator aligns everything to 16.
- No detection of invalid frees, no canaries, no junk filling: the OpenBSD
  allocator aborts on a pointer it does not know; mimalloc (built with
  MI_SECURE=0, no debug) may corrupt its lists instead. libroot_debug.so
  remains the tool for that.
- A thread that is killed (kill_thread()) does not run the exit hook: its
  pages stay assigned to it. Blocks in them can be freed by other threads,
  but nothing hands those pages to another thread (not tested).
- No strong random source at start (mimalloc's free list encoding is off
  with MI_SECURE=0, so this only seeds its heap tags).
- arm64: builds; see the issue for what was run.
